<h2>The identity fork</h2>
<p>Fork 1 (a) preserves subject identity, revision and evidence scope independently of encoding. Multiple representations, including typed records, namespaced strings and compatibility adapters, may coexist if they preserve those distinctions and keep non-model evidence outside model graduation. Fork 1 (b)'s synthetic model identities and Fork 1 (c)'s mutable aliases are rejected only where they conflate subjects, erase revision boundaries or admit ineligible evidence. <strong>Recommend Fork 1 (a)</strong>; the JSON shape is illustrative rather than the only permitted encoding.</p>
<p>The card gives a proposed planner shadow-entry JSON shape and the real model <code>readRegistry</code>/<code>statusFor</code> compatibility call. No generic registry API is claimed shipped. Preserve old persisted declarations and caller results during migration; reject conflicting identities and unsupported schema versions visibly.</p>
<h2>Supported by default</h2>
<ul>
  <li>One logical, versioned registry; optional storage partitions and a model facade, with one editable authority.</li>
  <li>Models, systems and rulings as distinct subject types, each with its own evidence criteria.</li>
  <li>One scheduled watcher and the same manual review routine, with shared singleton ownership and durable filing receipts.</li>
  <li>Tripwires file review cards without changing settings, disabling systems, promoting models or reopening rulings.</li>
  <li>launchd/systemd adapters and durable jobs for slow work; no full sweep inside a build-dispatch tick.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Evidence and operator review</h2>
<p>Enrollment declares metrics, baseline, tripwires, review timing and exit criteria. A collecting planner shadow baseline can be enrolled but cannot justify a comparative pass. Activation pins its evidence range and starts a persistent 30-day review clock in ET. Model delivery's 20-trial floor, informative trial, independent critical-miss veto and comparable rating remain model-specific.</p>
<p>Missing, stale, truncated or wrong-revision evidence produces an insufficient-evidence finding. Proposed sweep bounds are 100 subjects and 8 MiB input, with visible continuation and no remote calls during evaluation. Deduplication keys include subject, revision, criterion and evidence window. An operator review records continue, revise or exit; the watcher never makes that disposition itself. Under the <a href="/docs/agent/platform-decisions.md#supervision-no-borrowed-evidence">no-borrowed-evidence rule</a>, a recorded equivalence assessment cannot authorize cross-model or cross-triple graduation credit: each exact tuple must earn its own eligible record.</p>
<h2>Classification and related work</h2>
<p>This is repository governance tooling, not a new browser standard. <a href="/docs/agent/platform-decisions.md#model-probation-graduation-criteria">Model graduation</a> supplies narrower precedents for exact-identity evidence and role-specific criteria; <a href="/docs/agent/platform-decisions.md#planner-build-plan-and-execute">planner clause 9</a> authorizes the planner's review and operator exit only; daemon-jobs grounds execution mechanics. Extending these policies to other systems and rulings is proposed here, not already settled by those precedents. The open advisory-seat exit proposal #6782 includes automatic pausing; that is not standing authority for a generic watcher and must remain a separate spending-control ruling.</p>
